Subject: Defer Atom Interning For Lazy Bytecode

What this does
  Records each serialized non-constant atom's payload position during a lazy
  read and interns that atom on first use. The shared lazy source memoizes the
  result so nested functions reuse the same atom table.

Why we need it
  Large Metro bundles contain names used only by cold function bodies. Avoiding
  their string and symbol construction during the initial read reduces startup
  work while preserving eager validation of atom types, lengths, and boundaries.

What it adds
  Deferred atom offsets, bounded first-use atom parsing, memoized lookup, and
  cleanup for partially populated atom tables. Eager reads retain their current
  behavior, and copied lazy loading remains the React Native default.

Measured effect
  On the stripped Metro load-only fixture
  (SHA-256 57de13e7f0fa8572baac450ea5fb78137059c97b7be7e998097161fb003cd7f7),
  the host comparison used 3 warmups and 15 measured release samples with the
  same bytecode and compiler. Lazy loading with deferred atoms had a 0.670 ms
  median eval time versus 1.000 ms with eager atom interning, a 33.0% lower
  median. On a OnePlus 9 (Android 14, arm64-v8a), 20 cold launches per arm
  with the first discarded measured 211.829 ms versus 215.898 ms for
  appLoaded, while bundleLoaded medians were 70.857 ms versus 70.121 ms.
  The atom optimization therefore improved total startup in that run without
  improving the isolated bundle-loaded marker.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No additional bump. This optimization uses the framed function records from
  0031 and keeps BC_VERSION at 28.

